<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dinder River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dinder</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dinder River is a seasonal tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Blue Nile</a> that rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-highlands">Ethiopian highlands</a> west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tana">Lake Tana</a> and joins the Blue <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> below <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-sultanate-of-sennar">Sennar</a>. In 1744 Funj forces defeated an invading army of the Ethiopian emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iyasu-ii">Iyasu II</a> on its banks. Much of its lower course now lies within Dinder National Park.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dinder-river.jpg" alt="Dinder River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: محسن الفكي · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AF%D9%86%D8%AF%D8%B1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
